<main>

<header class="masthead">
  <p class="eyebrow">Battery polarity inspection · Station handbook</p>
  <h1>Pole Position</h1>
  <p class="standfirst">How to run this station, wire it to the PLC, teach it a new battery, and train the model that reads the terminal stamps.</p>
  <dl class="docmeta">
    <div><dt>Applies to</dt><dd>v0.24.0</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Document</dt><dd>Rev A</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Status</dt><dd>Unapproved draft</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Replaces</dt><dd>Nothing</dd></div>
  </dl>
</header>

<section class="part" id="safety">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">00</span><h2>What this station is for</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">Read this page before you use anything else in this handbook. It is short on purpose.</p>

  <p>Pole Position looks at a battery through a camera and decides whether the terminals are the right way round. It checks three things on each terminal: the mark stamped on top of the post, the color of the metal, and whether a red ring is present when the part is supposed to have one.</p>

  <p>It sends the PLC one of two answers: <span class="pill pass">Pass</span> or <span class="pill fail">Fail</span>. Nothing else. Every kind of problem &mdash; a reversed battery, a missing part, a camera that did not take a picture, a fault inside the software &mdash; comes out as the same Fail.</p>

  <div class="note stop">
    <span class="tag">This is not a safety device</span>
    <p>Pole Position is a quality inspection tool. It is not a safety PLC, a guard, or an emergency stop. The PLC owns every decision about stopping the line, rejecting a part, and allowing a bypass. If the station is switched off, the line must still be safe.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>It is built to fail closed</h3>
  <p>&ldquo;Fail closed&rdquo; means the station only says Pass when everything worked. A part passes only when the camera took a fresh picture for that exact cycle, and every check the recipe asks for was completed and came back good.</p>
  <p>If any of these happen, the answer is Fail:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>There is no recipe, or the recipe has no reference picture.</li>
    <li>The picture is old, missing, or too poor to judge.</li>
    <li>The station could not find the battery in the picture.</li>
    <li>The model is unsure about a stamp.</li>
    <li>A terminal mark, metal color, or red ring does not match the recipe.</li>
    <li>Something inside the software went wrong.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A Fail is never a guess. If the station cannot be sure, it does not pass the part.</p>
</section>

<section class="part" id="tour">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">01</span><h2>The seven screens</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">Buttons down the left side switch screens. Everything below is the same on every screen.</p>

  {{FIGURE:overview|The Overview screen after a part passed. The strip along the top and the strip along the bottom never change, whichever screen you are on.}}

  <h3>The strip along the top</h3>
  <p>This is the state of the machine right now.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The badge on the left</strong> says <code>READY</code>, <code>DEGRADED</code>, or <code>FAULT</code>. Ready means the station can inspect parts.</li>
    <li><strong>Active recipe</strong> is the battery the station is set up for, shown as a number and a name.</li>
    <li><strong>Part count, Pass, Fail, Reject rate</strong> are the counters for this run. A supervisor can reset them.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The strip along the bottom</h3>
  <p>This is the health of the equipment: the camera, the lighting, the PLC connection, free disk space, and an overall system health word. Anything in red here needs attention, even if parts are still being inspected.</p>

  <h3>What each screen is for</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Screen</th><th>Use it to</th><th>Who uses it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Overview</strong></td><td>Watch parts being inspected and see the last result</td><td>Operator</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Inspection</strong></td><td>Look closely at one result, terminal by terminal</td><td>Operator, quality</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Recipes</strong></td><td>Add or change the setup for a battery</td><td>Technician</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>ML Train</strong></td><td>Collect pictures of terminal tops and train the model</td><td>Technician, engineering</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Diagnostics</strong></td><td>Check the camera, disk, and system health in detail</td><td>Technician, maintenance</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Events</strong></td><td>Read the history of what happened and who changed what</td><td>Quality, supervisor</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Settings</strong></td><td>Set up the camera, the PLC, storage, and the model</td><td>Technician, controls</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="part" id="operate">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">02</span><h2>Running the station</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">This part is for the person at the station. You do not need to know anything about the software to follow it.</p>

  <h3 id="operate-start">Starting a shift</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Turn the station on and wait for the Overview screen.</li>
    <li>Look at the badge in the top left. It should say <code>READY</code>.</li>
    <li>Look at the active recipe. It must be the battery you are actually running.</li>
    <li>Look at the strip along the bottom. Camera, PLC, and system health should not be red.</li>
    <li>Run one known-good part and confirm it passes. Run one known-bad part and confirm it rejects.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">Do this every shift</span>
    <p>The known-good and known-bad check takes under a minute and is the only thing that proves the station is really looking at your parts. A station can say <code>READY</code> and still be set up for the wrong battery.</p>
  </div>

  <p>If the badge says <code>DEGRADED</code> or <code>FAULT</code>, or the recipe is wrong, do not start production. Go to <a href="#operate-stop">When to stop and call for help</a>.</p>

  <h3 id="operate-read">Reading a result</h3>
  <p>After each part, the big box on the right shows the answer.</p>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What it says</th><th>What it means</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><span class="pill pass">Pass</span></td><td>Every check the recipe asked for came back good.</td><td>Nothing. The part carries on.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><span class="pill fail">Reject</span></td><td>At least one check failed. The reason is written under the box.</td><td>See below.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Not ready</strong></td><td>The station could not inspect. No recipe, no reference picture, or the model is not installed.</td><td>Call a technician. Do not run parts.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>System fault</strong></td><td>Something went wrong inside the station, often the camera.</td><td>Call a technician. Do not run parts.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Under the answer you also get the exact time the picture was taken, the frame number, how long the inspection took, and whether the picture quality was good. If a supervisor ever asks &ldquo;which part was that?&rdquo;, the cycle number identifies it.</p>

  <h3 id="operate-reject">When a part rejects</h3>
  <p>The picture shows you where the problem is. The terminal that caused the reject is drawn in a <strong>red box with a thick line</strong> and labelled <code>REJECT</code>. A terminal that was fine keeps its normal blue box.</p>

  {{FIGURE:overview-reject|A reversed battery. Both terminals are boxed in red and labelled REJECT, and the reason line reads POLARITY MARKINGS REVERSED. In a normal reject only the terminal at fault turns red.}}

  <p>Read the reason line first, then look at which box is red.</p>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Reason you may see</th><th>In plain words</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>POLARITY MARKINGS REVERSED</code></td><td>The battery is in backwards.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TERMINAL MARKING MISMATCH</code></td><td>The stamp on a post is not the one the recipe expects.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>TERMINAL FINISH MISMATCH</code></td><td>The metal is the wrong color &mdash; brass where silver was expected, or the other way round.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>RED RING MISSING</code></td><td>A post that must have a red ring does not have one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>NO DECISION</code> or low confidence</td><td>The station could not read the stamp well enough to be sure. Often glare, dirt, or a part sitting crooked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>NO NEW CAMERA FRAME</code></td><td>The camera did not give a fresh picture for this cycle.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h4>Steps to take</h4>
  <ol>
    <li>Take the part off the fixture and look at it yourself.</li>
    <li>If the part really is wrong, put it in the reject bin. The station did its job.</li>
    <li>If the part looks correct to you, do not run it again to get a pass. Set it aside, label it, and tell your supervisor.</li>
    <li>If several good parts in a row reject, stop and call a technician. Something has moved, the lighting has changed, or the recipe is wrong.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="note stop">
    <span class="tag">Never do this</span>
    <p>Do not re-run a part over and over until it passes. Do not move a part to a different position to make it pass. If a good part rejects, that is information a technician needs, not a nuisance to work around.</p>
  </div>

  <h4>Looking closer</h4>
  <p>Press <code>VIEW DETAILS</code>, or open the <strong>Inspection</strong> screen, to see one terminal at a time: the close-up picture, what the recipe expected, what the station saw, and how sure it was.</p>

  {{FIGURE:inspection-detail-reject|The Inspection screen for a rejected part. Each terminal gets its own card with the expected value, the detected value, and the confidence.}}

  <h4>Saving the evidence</h4>
  <p>For a rejected part you can press <code>EXPORT INSPECTION ZIP</code> to save a file containing the pictures and the details. Quality uses this to look at a problem later.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="tag">Why a pass cannot be exported</span>
    <p>The station saves pictures for rejects only. A passed part leaves no pictures behind on purpose &mdash; it keeps the disk from filling up with images nobody will ever look at. So the export button does nothing for a part that passed. That is normal, not a fault.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="operate-bypass">Bypass</h3>
  <p>Bypass is the amber button on the Overview screen. Turning it on tells the PLC that the inspection result is not allowed to stop the line.</p>
  <p>Bypass does <strong>not</strong> make parts pass. The camera keeps taking pictures, the station keeps checking, rejects are still recorded, and the real answer still goes to the PLC. All that changes is what the PLC does with the answer.</p>

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">Bypass is not yours to decide</span>
    <p>Only turn bypass on when your site's written bypass procedure says to, and when the person named in that procedure has approved it. Every time bypass is switched on or off it is written into the event log with the time and the user. Turn it back off as soon as the reason for it is over.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="operate-stop">When to stop and call for help</h3>
  <p>Stop running parts and call a technician if any of these are true:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The badge says <code>FAULT</code>, or the answer is <strong>Not ready</strong> or <strong>System fault</strong>.</li>
    <li>The active recipe is not the battery you are running.</li>
    <li>Camera or PLC in the bottom strip is red.</li>
    <li>A part you are sure is good rejects, and a second good part rejects too.</li>
    <li><strong>A part you are sure is bad passes.</strong></li>
    <li>Disk space in the bottom strip is low.</li>
    <li>The screen looks wrong &mdash; text on top of other text, or controls you cannot reach.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note stop">
    <span class="tag">A bad part that passes</span>
    <p>This is the most serious thing that can happen at this station, and it is more serious than any number of good parts rejecting. Stop the station. Keep the part exactly as it is and label it. Do not clear the screen, do not run more parts, and tell your supervisor and quality straight away.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="part" id="plc">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">03</span><h2>PLC commissioning</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">For the controls engineer. Pole Position talks to a Logix controller over pycomm3. It publishes a binary result, a heartbeat, and a bypass request, and it reads a trigger and a recipe selector.</p>

  <h3 id="plc-tags">Tag map</h3>
  <p>These are the default names. Every one of them is editable under <strong>Settings &rarr; PLC TAGS</strong>. Create them with the types below before connecting the station.</p>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Default tag</th><th>Type</th><th>Direction</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Inspection trigger</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Trigger</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>PLC &rarr; HMI</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HMI busy</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Busy</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>HMI &rarr; PLC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inspection complete</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Complete</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>HMI &rarr; PLC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inspection pass</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Pass</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>HMI &rarr; PLC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inspection fail</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Fail</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>HMI &rarr; PLC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Requested recipe</td><td><code>BatteryVision.RecipeName</code></td><td>STRING, or SINT/INT/DINT</td><td>PLC &rarr; HMI</td></tr>
        <tr><td>HMI heartbeat</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Heartbeat</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>HMI &rarr; PLC</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bypass request</td><td><code>BatteryVision.Bypass</code></td><td>BOOL</td><td>HMI &rarr; PLC, read back</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {{FIGURE:settings|The Settings screen. PLC tags, PLC mode and path, the heartbeat interval, camera setup, storage, and the installed model all live here.}}

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">Upgrading from before v0.18</span>
    <p>The old <code>BatteryVision.FailCode</code> DINT was replaced by the <code>BatteryVision.Fail</code> BOOL. Loading an older configuration migrates a tag ending in <code>FailCode</code> to the same name ending in <code>Fail</code>. Check that BOOL tag under <strong>Settings &rarr; PLC TAGS</strong> before you reconnect a production controller.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="plc-sequence">Cycle sequencing</h3>
  <p>One inspection runs like this. The station owns the three output bits; the PLC owns the trigger.</p>

  <div class="diagram">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 720 300" role="img" aria-labelledby="timing-title timing-desc">
      <title id="timing-title">Inspection cycle timing</title>
      <desc id="timing-desc">Trigger rises and the station raises Busy. Busy stays high for the inspection. When the inspection finishes, Busy falls and Complete rises together with exactly one of Pass or Fail, and those stay latched until the next trigger edge is accepted and Busy rises again.</desc>

      <line class="gridline" x1="150" y1="26" x2="150" y2="268"></line>
      <line class="gridline" x1="300" y1="26" x2="300" y2="268"></line>
      <line class="gridline" x1="470" y1="26" x2="470" y2="268"></line>
      <line class="gridline" x1="620" y1="26" x2="620" y2="268"></line>

      <text class="stage" x="150" y="18">Rising edge</text>
      <text class="stage" x="300" y="18">Grading</text>
      <text class="stage" x="470" y="18">Result latched</text>
      <text class="stage" x="620" y="18">Next cycle</text>

      <text class="sig" x="8" y="52">Trigger</text>
      <path class="wire wire-in" d="M96 60 L150 60 L150 34 L240 34 L240 60 L598 60 L598 34 L688 34 L688 60 L704 60"></path>

      <text class="sig" x="8" y="110">Busy</text>
      <path class="wire wire-out" d="M96 118 L152 118 L152 92 L470 92 L470 118 L620 118 L620 92 L704 92"></path>

      <text class="sig" x="8" y="168">Complete</text>
      <path class="wire wire-out" d="M96 176 L470 176 L470 150 L620 150 L620 176 L704 176"></path>

      <text class="sig" x="8" y="226">Pass</text>
      <path class="wire wire-pass" d="M96 234 L470 234 L470 208 L620 208 L620 234 L704 234"></path>

      <text class="sig" x="8" y="284">Fail</text>
      <path class="wire wire-fail" d="M96 292 L704 292"></path>

      <line class="axis" x1="96" y1="26" x2="96" y2="296"></line>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption><b>Figure</b><span>A passing cycle. A rejecting cycle is identical except that <code>Fail</code> rises instead of <code>Pass</code>. Exactly one of the two is ever high while <code>Complete</code> is high.</span></figcaption>

  <h4>Step by step</h4>
  <ol>
    <li>The PLC sets <code>Trigger</code> high. The station acts on the <strong>rising edge</strong> only.</li>
    <li>The station accepts the edge, sets <code>Busy</code> high, and clears the previous result.</li>
    <li>The station takes a fresh picture and grades it. <code>Busy</code> stays high for the whole time.</li>
    <li>The station drops <code>Busy</code>, raises <code>Complete</code>, and raises exactly one of <code>Pass</code> or <code>Fail</code>.</li>
    <li>The PLC reads the result while <code>Complete</code> is high, and drops <code>Trigger</code>.</li>
    <li>The result stays latched until the next accepted cycle raises <code>Busy</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Cycle phase</th><th class="num">Busy</th><th class="num">Complete</th><th class="num">Pass</th><th class="num">Fail</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Idle, result cleared</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Inspection running</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Completed PASS</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Completed non-PASS</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">1</td><td class="num">0</td><td class="num">1</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Reject, not ready, acquisition failure, and internal fault all publish the same <code>Fail</code>. No reason code reaches the controller; reasons live in the HMI and in the retained evidence.</p>

  <div class="note stop">
    <span class="tag">Three things your program must not assume</span>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>There is no acknowledgement tag and no sequence number.</strong> Treat <code>Complete</code> as the validity of the latest result. Do not wait for the station to clear it after <code>Trigger</code> falls &mdash; it will not.</li>
      <li><strong><code>Trigger</code> must be observed low before a new rising edge is accepted.</strong> Holding it high does not produce repeat inspections.</li>
      <li><strong>A trigger can produce no result at all.</strong> If the requested recipe does not match the active one, the station logs the mismatch and ignores that edge. It does not publish a synthetic Fail. Your program must inhibit the product and apply its own timeout when <code>Busy</code> and <code>Complete</code> do not follow a request.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>When PLC settings are connected or applied, the station writes the idle row of the table above. That clears stale outputs and proves all four output tags exist and accept BOOL writes before the backend is declared usable.</p>

  <h3 id="plc-heartbeat">Heartbeat and watchdog</h3>
  <p>The station toggles <code>Heartbeat</code> every 1000 ms by default. It keeps toggling while a camera or vision cycle is busy, so it is a true liveness signal and not a measure of load.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="tag">Watch the transition, not the value</span>
    <p>The heartbeat value itself means nothing. Only a periodic change proves the station's task is alive. A program that reads the bit as healthy or unhealthy will believe a dead station is fine half the time.</p>
  </div>

  <pre><code>IF BatteryVision.Heartbeat &lt;&gt; Last_HMI_Heartbeat THEN
    Last_HMI_Heartbeat := BatteryVision.Heartbeat;
    HMI_Watchdog_Reset := TRUE;
ELSE
    HMI_Watchdog_Reset := FALSE;
END_IF;

// Implement the site-standard timer around HMI_Watchdog_Reset.
// At the default 1000 ms heartbeat, a 3000-4000 ms timeout is recommended.</code></pre>

  <p>Declare communications unhealthy when no change is seen for at least three times the configured interval. A failed heartbeat write is treated by the station as a PLC communication fault; it does <strong>not</strong> switch itself to simulation. Select simulation explicitly on the PLC tab when working without a controller.</p>

  <h3 id="plc-recipe">Recipe selection</h3>
  <p>Every recipe has a unique name and a stable positive number. Under <strong>Settings &rarr; PLC TAGS</strong>, choose which the selector tag carries:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recipe name</strong> &mdash; the tag is read as a Logix STRING.</li>
    <li><strong>Recipe number</strong> &mdash; the tag is read as SINT, INT, or DINT. Numbers do not change across revisions, which makes them the safer choice for a program that outlives a recipe edit.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The active recipe must match the requested name or number before a trigger is accepted. A mismatch is logged and the edge is ignored, as described above.</p>

  <h3 id="plc-bypass">Bypass logic</h3>
  <p>Bypass is a request from the station to the controller, written to <code>BatteryVision.Bypass</code> and read back to confirm. The station reads the tag continuously, so a PLC-side change appears in the HMI.</p>
  <p>Bypass is deliberately not a forced pass. Acquisition continues, inspection continues, non-PASS evidence is still recorded, and the real result tags are still published. Only the controller's use of the result changes.</p>

  <pre><code>Bypass_Effective := BatteryVision.Bypass AND HMI_Comm_OK;
Inspection_Interlock_OK := Bypass_Effective OR BatteryVision.Pass;</code></pre>

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">Condition bypass on the watchdog</span>
    <p>Gating bypass with the heartbeat watchdog matters: a network or station failure must not leave a stale TRUE bypass request effective forever. The station tries to clear the tag on a normal shutdown, but the PLC watchdog is the only real protection against a stale request. Use your facility's normal permissive structure rather than copying the expression above into safety-related logic.</p>
  </div>

  <h3 id="plc-steps">Commissioning steps</h3>
  <p>Work through these against the real controller and the real program revision. Record what you observe, not what is configured.</p>
  <ol class="check">
    <li>Create all tags with the expected types.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Settings &rarr; PLC TAGS</strong>, choose name or number selection, and confirm every tag name.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Settings &rarr; PLC MODE</strong> and set the Logix path.</li>
    <li>Set the heartbeat interval. The default is 1000 ms.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>APPLY &amp; TEST PYCOMM3</strong>.</li>
    <li>Confirm the heartbeat tag changes state about once per interval.</li>
    <li>Confirm the PLC watchdog stays healthy while an inspection is running.</li>
    <li>From Overview, enable bypass and verify <code>BatteryVision.Bypass</code> becomes TRUE in Logix.</li>
    <li>Disable bypass and verify the tag becomes FALSE.</li>
    <li>Trigger a known pass and verify <code>Complete=1, Pass=1, Fail=0</code>.</li>
    <li>Trigger a known reject and verify <code>Complete=1, Pass=0, Fail=1</code>.</li>
    <li>Trigger with bypass on and confirm the station still evaluates the real result while the controller bypasses the quality interlock.</li>
    <li>Request a recipe the station does not have. Confirm the product stays inhibited and your timeout logic fires.</li>
    <li>Pull the network cable mid-cycle. Confirm the line ends up safe and the watchdog trips.</li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="part" id="recipe">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">04</span><h2>Recipe setup</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">A recipe is everything the station knows about one battery: what it looks like, where the terminals are, and what each terminal must show. For a technician.</p>

  {{FIGURE:recipes|The Recipes screen. Each row is a battery. The panel shows the selected recipe's revision, validation state, and reference image.}}

  <h3>What a recipe holds</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Part of the recipe</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Reference picture</td><td>A photo of a known-good battery, taken by this camera on this fixture. Everything else is measured against it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Battery outline</td><td>Where the battery sits in the picture. Used to find the battery even when it moves or turns.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Terminal search areas</td><td>Where to look for each post.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Marking circle</td><td>The area inside each post crop that holds the stamp.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expected marking</td><td><code>PLUS</code>, <code>MINUS</code>, or <code>BLANK</code> for that post.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Expected finish</td><td><code>SILVER</code> or <code>BRASS</code> for that post.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Red ring required</td><td>Whether that post must have a red ring.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Validation record</td><td>Proof that real parts were inspected with this exact setup.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Recipes are never edited in place</h3>
  <p>Editing revision 2 creates revision 3. Revision 2 stays in the database exactly as it was. The active revision keeps running while you build the draft, and only one revision is active at a time.</p>
  <p>Any change to a taught area, an expected value, or a vision setting clears the validation record for that draft. That is deliberate: validation evidence belongs to one exact configuration, tracked by a checksum. Change the configuration and the old evidence no longer describes what the station will do.</p>

  <h3>Building a revision</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <strong>Recipes</strong> and select the battery.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>EDIT / NEW REVISION</strong>, or double-click the row.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>CAPTURE NEW REFERENCE</strong>, which is recommended, or <strong>KEEP EXISTING REFERENCE</strong>. Keeping the old one is always an explicit choice; it is never assumed.</li>
    <li>If capturing, review the fresh frame, retake if needed, and select <strong>USE THIS IMAGE</strong>.</li>
    <li>Confirm the battery outline and the orientation reference.</li>
    <li>Teach the negative and positive terminal search areas.</li>
    <li>Adjust the dashed marking circle inside each enlarged terminal crop.</li>
    <li>Set the expected marking, the visible finish, and the red-ring requirement for each terminal.</li>
    <li>Run the required validation samples, moving or turning the known-good battery between samples.</li>
    <li>Review the results, then save as a draft, or activate if every gate passed.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="note stop">
    <span class="tag">Check what was actually saved</span>
    <p>Builds before v0.24.0 could clear a terminal's red-ring requirement when a recipe was reopened for editing. A cleared requirement is invisible on the inspection screen and turns a part that should reject into a pass. After building or editing any recipe, confirm what is stored:</p>
    <pre><code>python scripts\diagnose_station.py --station "C:\ProgramData\Pole Position"</code></pre>
    <p>Check the red-ring flag and the expected finish printed for each terminal against the real part. Do this for every recipe created or edited before v0.24.0.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>The reference picture</h3>
  <p>The reference must be a controlled known-good battery. A bad reference teaches the station the wrong finish or the wrong marking, and every part after it is judged against that mistake. Your site's recipe approval process is what prevents this; the software cannot.</p>
  <p>Each capture stores its frame identity, timestamp, resolution, camera backend and profile, quality measurements, and a SHA-256. An imported recipe never trusts an outside file as station evidence &mdash; it forces a fresh capture on this station.</p>

  <h3>Placing the marking circle</h3>
  <p>The marking circle is a search area, not a template. Threaded posts turn independently of the battery case, so the station finds the round top inside the circle, measures the stamp angle, and squares it up before comparing.</p>
  <p>Place the circle so that it:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>contains the whole round terminal top at every position the part can sit in;</li>
    <li>leaves a little margin so the circle finder has room;</li>
    <li>excludes as much washer, hex, and ring geometry as you can manage;</li>
    <li>clearly shows the actual <code>+</code> or <code>&minus;</code> in the enlarged crop.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The Inspection screen reports how the terminal top was used as <code>NOMINAL</code>, <code>CONDITIONAL ACCEPT</code>, or <code>FALLBACK</code>. You do not configure the thresholds behind those words; you improve them by placing the circle well and validating across real position and rotation variation.</p>

  <h3>Silver and brass</h3>
  <p>Finish is a recipe property, not something the model reads. The station compares the current terminal-top crop with the same terminal in the accepted reference, using color measurements that ignore dark stamp grooves and bright glare. A confident shift to the opposite material fails. So does an ambiguous result &mdash; it fails closed and keeps a diagnostic image showing current against reference.</p>
  <p>A recipe from before v0.21 shows finish as <code>NOT CONFIGURED</code> and keeps working. The moment you open it for a new revision, you must choose both finishes and complete fresh validation before that revision can activate.</p>

  <h3>Validation</h3>
  <p>Validation runs the real thing: the same camera acquisition, the same locator, the same classifier, the same ring detector, the same evidence writer. Only PLC publication and production counters are held back.</p>
  <p>A sample counts only when the battery's pose is meaningfully different from the samples already accepted. Turning and shifting the part between samples is the point, not a formality &mdash; it is what proves the setup tolerates the way parts really arrive.</p>
  <p>A draft cannot be activated until the locator, the classifier, and the real validation gates all pass.</p>
</section>

<section class="part" id="ml">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">05</span><h2>ML training</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">The model reads the stamp on a terminal top. This part covers collecting pictures, training, and putting a model into service. For a technician or engineer.</p>

  <h3>What the model does and does not do</h3>
  <p>The model sees one thing: the isolated metal terminal top. It answers with one of four classes.</p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>PLUS</code></td><td>The stamp is a valid plus.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MINUS</code></td><td>The stamp is a valid minus.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BLANK</code></td><td>The face is present with no stamp.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>INVALID MARKING</code></td><td>The face is present but the pattern is not an acceptable plus, minus, or blank &mdash; damaged, partial, malformed, doubled, or unrelated.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Which post is physically positive or negative, and whether a red ring is present, are separate recipe checks. The model never sees them.</p>

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">Two things INVALID MARKING is not for</span>
    <p>Do not use it for glare, blur, or bad exposure &mdash; those are <code>NO DECISION</code>, and the station handles them by failing closed. Do not use it for a missing terminal &mdash; a separate physical-input gate owns that. Using it for either teaches the model to accept pictures it should refuse.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>One dataset for the whole station</h3>
  <p>The dataset is global across recipes, not per battery. Collect terminal tops from every battery family, supplier, terminal construction, finish, stamp depth, and condition the station may ever see. When a new family arrives later, keep everything you already have and retrain on the accumulated set &mdash; never on the new family alone.</p>

  <h3>Step 1 &mdash; Capture</h3>

  {{FIGURE:ml-1-capture|The capture step. The whole camera frame stays visible with a labelled dashed circle on each terminal top. There is no separate crop preview, so you can confirm every circle at once.}}

  <ol>
    <li>Put a representative battery under the real camera.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>CAPTURE FRESH FRAME</strong>.</li>
    <li>Two dashed circles are created for you, for the usual two-post battery.</li>
    <li>Move, or <strong>REDRAW ACTIVE CIRCLE</strong> around, the first flat metal face.</li>
    <li>Set its real class.</li>
    <li>Select the second circle, place it, and set its class.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>+ ADD CIRCLE</strong> if more terminal tops are visible in the same frame.</li>
    <li>Optionally enter a family tag such as <code>Group31 / supplier A</code>. It is a label only and does not affect the class.</li>
    <li>Select <strong>SAVE ALL CIRCLES</strong>. Every circle is validated before any sample is committed.</li>
    <li>Move or turn the battery and capture another fresh frame.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="note stop">
    <span class="tag">The circle rule</span>
    <p>Each circle must contain the flat metal face and its stamp, and nothing else. Keep all of these <em>outside</em> the circle: the red polarity ring, the moulded or printed case <code>+</code>/<code>&minus;</code>, the washer, the outer hex hardware, and unrelated case geometry.</p>
    <p>If the red ring is inside the circle, the model can learn to read the ring instead of the stamp. It will look excellent in training and it will pass reversed batteries in production. There is deliberately no red-ring checkbox anywhere on this screen for the same reason.</p>
  </div>

  <p>The circle becomes a square image. Pixels outside the circle are replaced with the median color measured inside it. Training and production both use this same <code>taught_circle_masked_square_v1</code> rule, so what the model trains on is exactly what it will be given in production.</p>
  <p>All crops from one frame share a capture-group ID and are forced into the same train, validation, or test split. That is what stops a picture of one terminal appearing in both training and validation and flattering the score.</p>

  <h3>Step 2 &mdash; Review</h3>

  {{FIGURE:ml-2-review|The review step. Six stored crops per page, filterable by class and by family, with the label and the remove control on each card.}}

  <p>Browse everything the station has stored. Filter by class or family, correct a wrong label, or remove a bad crop. Edits are written to the sample manifest straight away. The header shows the total, how many independent camera frames they came from, and which family tags are represented.</p>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Class</th><th class="num">Advisory target</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>PLUS</code></td><td class="num">100+ independent captures</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>MINUS</code></td><td class="num">100+ independent captures</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>BLANK</code></td><td class="num">100+ independent captures</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>INVALID MARKING</code></td><td class="num">100+ independent captures</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>These are targets, not gates. Nothing blocks you at a low count &mdash; training needs only one labelled example per class and a leakage-safe validation group. But a station qualified on thin data is a station that has not yet met the part that breaks it. Keep accumulating batteries, suppliers and lots, stamp angles, finishes, positions, lighting, scratches, oxidation, and a genuine variety of invalid patterns.</p>

  <h3>Step 3 &mdash; Prepare</h3>

  {{FIGURE:ml-3-prepare|The prepare step, with the dataset builder and the training-runtime check.}}

  <p><strong>PREPARE DATASET</strong> builds leakage-safe <code>train</code>, <code>val</code>, and <code>test</code> folders under the station's runtime directory. You never need to find or edit those folders.</p>
  <p><strong>CHECK TRAINING RUNTIME</strong> verifies Ultralytics, PyTorch, ONNX export, ONNX Runtime, and CUDA. NVIDIA hardware is reported separately from PyTorch's CUDA capability, so a CPU-only PyTorch on a GPU workstation is obvious rather than mysterious.</p>

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">If CUDA is missing on a GPU workstation</span>
    <p>The bundled PyTorch decides this, not the machine. A build produced without a CUDA index carries a CPU-only PyTorch, and training will run correctly but far more slowly. Check <code>cuda_version</code> in <code>BUILD-MANIFEST.json</code> beside the application. Rebuilding with the matching CUDA index is the fix.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Step 4 &mdash; Train</h3>

  {{FIGURE:ml-4-train|The training step. Base model, epochs, image size, batch size, device, and the model ID and version that the recipe will later bind to.}}

  <p>Choose the base model or a local <code>.pt</code> file, the epochs, the image size, the batch size, the device, and a model ID and version. <strong>START MODEL TRAINING</strong> runs in the background, and normal inspection cycles are held while it does.</p>
  <p>The worker trains with full rotation augmentation, exports the best model to ONNX, and evaluates it on the held-out test split using the same confidence and margin logic production uses.</p>

  <h3>Step 5 &mdash; Deploy</h3>

  {{FIGURE:ml-5-deploy|The deploy step, showing held-out performance before anything is installed.}}

  <p>You are shown the held-out image count, the acceptance rate, accuracy including low-confidence abstentions, accuracy over accepted results only, and recall for each class.</p>
  <p><strong>INSTALL CANDIDATE FOR RECIPE VALIDATION</strong> becomes available once the exported package passes the normal production loader and runtime self-test. If held-out class coverage or performance is below target, the station asks for an explicit confirmation first.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <span class="tag">Installing a model changes nothing on its own</span>
    <p>An installed model does not make any recipe valid and does not touch an active revision. Open <strong>Recipes</strong>, select the battery, and choose <strong>CREATE ML REVISION</strong>. The new revision binds to that exact model ID, version, and SHA-256, and must pass guided physical validation before it can be activated.</p>
  </div>

  <p>If training exported the ONNX successfully but failed afterwards because ONNX Runtime was missing, the candidate is kept. Repair the runtime and select <strong>LATEST CANDIDATE</strong> rather than training again.</p>

  <h4>Rules that protect the data</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Capture needs a real camera. Demo images and camera fallback cannot create training samples.</li>
    <li>Every circle from one frame is saved as one logical batch, and all circles are validated before any of them is committed.</li>
    <li>Identical image bytes in the same class are not counted twice.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="part" id="care">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">06</span><h2>Daily care and recovery</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">Keeping the station healthy, and getting it back when something goes wrong.</p>

  <h3>What to check, and how often</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>How often</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Every shift</td><td>Badge reads <code>READY</code>; the active recipe is the battery being run; one known-good and one known-bad part behave correctly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Every shift</td><td>Nothing red in the bottom strip.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Weekly</td><td>Free disk space on Diagnostics. Clean the lens and check the light has not moved or dimmed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Weekly</td><td>Skim the Events screen for changes nobody expected.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>After any change</td><td>Take a workstation backup.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  {{FIGURE:diagnostics|The Diagnostics screen: camera state, measured disk space, and system health in detail.}}

  {{FIGURE:events|The Events screen. Every recipe change, activation, model install, and bypass switch is recorded with the time and the user.}}

  <h3>Backups</h3>
  <p>A backup holds the configuration, the recipes and their revision history, the reference images, the validation evidence, the installed model, and the training samples. Take one after any recipe or model change, and before any Windows work on the machine.</p>
  <p>To see what is actually inside one:</p>
  <pre><code>python scripts\analyze_station_backup.py "path\to\backup.zip"</code></pre>
  <p>It separates what a replacement station genuinely needs from what it can rebuild &mdash; prepared datasets, past training runs, uncommitted captures. If a backup is far larger than you expect, that report tells you why.</p>

  <div class="note care">
    <span class="tag">Rehearse the restore</span>
    <p>A backup nobody has ever restored is a guess, not a recovery plan. Restore one onto a spare workstation, confirm the recipes and model came across, and confirm the red-ring and finish settings survived using <code>diagnose_station.py</code>. Write down how long it took.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>If a restore gets stuck</h3>
  <p>Older builds left a pending-import flag behind when a restore failed, so the station retried the same failing restore at every launch and refused any other backup. Reinstalling does not clear it, because station data lives apart from the program files.</p>
  <pre><code>python scripts\clear_pending_restore.py
python scripts\clear_pending_restore.py --clear</code></pre>
  <p>Run it without arguments first to see what is there. Clearing removes only the flag and its staged copy; recipes, models, evidence, and configuration are untouched.</p>

  <h3>Troubleshooting</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What you see</th><th>Most likely cause</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Every part passes, including bad ones</td><td>The camera backend is set to simulation, so the station is grading a bundled demo picture instead of the part. Or a recipe's checks were cleared.</td><td>Stop production. Run <code>diagnose_station.py</code>; it reports simulation first.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Good parts reject in a run</td><td>Lighting changed, the lens moved, the fixture shifted, or a new supplier's finish differs.</td><td>Compare a current picture with the recipe reference. Re-teach and revalidate if the part has genuinely changed.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>NO NEW CAMERA FRAME</code></td><td>The camera did not deliver a picture for that cycle.</td><td>Check the camera on Diagnostics and the cable. This is a fault, not a reject.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>PLC red in the bottom strip</td><td>Path, tag names, or network.</td><td>Settings &rarr; PLC, then <strong>APPLY &amp; TEST PYCOMM3</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Text overlapping on screen</td><td>A display scale that leaves too little room.</td><td>v0.24.0 scrolls instead of overlapping. If you still see overlap, report it with the monitor resolution and the Windows scale setting.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Training fails to start</td><td>Training runtime incomplete.</td><td>ML Train &rarr; Prepare &rarr; <strong>CHECK TRAINING RUNTIME</strong>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Backup or file operations stall</td><td>Antivirus scanning every file the station touches.</td><td>Exclude the program directory and <code>C:\ProgramData\Pole Position</code> from real-time scanning.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Where things are kept</h3>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Program files</td><td><code>C:\Program Files\Pole Position\</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Everything the station owns</td><td><code>C:\ProgramData\Pole Position\</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Settings</td><td><code>...\config.json</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Recipes and history</td><td><code>...\runtime\battery_inspector.db</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reject evidence</td><td><code>...\runtime\inspections\</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Installed model</td><td><code>...\models\</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Training samples</td><td><code>...\runtime\ml_training\</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>The installer leaves the station data directory alone on purpose, so reinstalling never destroys recipes or evidence &mdash; and never clears a problem that lives in that data.</p>
</section>

<section class="part" id="glossary">
  <div class="part-head"><span class="part-num">07</span><h2>Words used here</h2></div>
  <p class="part-intro">Plain meanings for the terms on the screens.</p>

  <dl class="glossary">
    <div><dt>Recipe</dt><dd>Everything the station knows about one battery: the reference picture, where the terminals are, and what each one must show.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Revision</dt><dd>One saved version of a recipe. Editing makes a new one; old ones are kept and never changed.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Reference picture</dt><dd>The photo of a known-good battery that every later part is measured against.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Validation</dt><dd>Running real parts through a new recipe to prove it works before it is allowed to run production.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Active recipe</dt><dd>The one recipe the station is using right now. Only one at a time.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Terminal top</dt><dd>The flat metal face on top of a battery post, where the stamp is.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Marking</dt><dd>The stamp on the terminal top: plus, minus, or nothing.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Finish</dt><dd>The color of the metal on the terminal top: silver or brass.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Red ring</dt><dd>The red ring around a post on batteries that use one to show polarity.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>ROI</dt><dd>Region of interest. A box or circle drawn on a picture to say &ldquo;look here&rdquo;.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Model</dt><dd>The trained file that reads a stamp and says plus, minus, blank, or invalid.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Confidence</dt><dd>How sure the model is. Below the recipe's limit, the station refuses to decide instead of guessing.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Fail closed</dt><dd>When in doubt, do not pass the part.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Bypass</dt><dd>Telling the PLC not to let the inspection result stop the line. It does not make parts pass.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Heartbeat</dt><dd>A bit the station keeps flipping so the PLC can tell it is still alive.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>The pictures and details saved for a rejected part.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Backup</dt><dd>A single file holding everything this station owns, so it can be rebuilt.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>

